Package: SIV
Type: Package
Title: Separable Instrumental Variable Methods for Treatment Effects and
        Nonignorable Missing Data
Version: 0.1.1
Authors@R: c(
    person("Chan", "Park", role = c("aut", "cre"),
           email = "parkchan@illinois.edu"),
    person("Eric J.", "Tchetgen Tchetgen", role = "aut"))
Description: Implements the nonparametric separable instrumental variable (SIV)
    estimator of average and quantile treatment effects on the treated (ATT and
    QTT), together with an influence-function falsification test, under a
    logit-separable binary treatment choice model (Park and Tchetgen Tchetgen),
    together with faithful implementations of the competing estimators of
    Liu et al. (2021) <arXiv:1506.08149>, Sun et al. (2021)
    <doi:10.5705/ss.202016.0324> and Sun, Miao and Wickramarachchi (2025)
    <doi:10.5705/ss.202023.0383>.  All competing estimators are M-estimators
    and are supplied with their stacked-estimating-equation ("sandwich")
    covariance estimators.  The proposed estimator supports both binary and
    continuous outcomes through an explicit 'y_type' argument.  For a continuous
    outcome, the default baseline density combines cross-fitted 'np' and
    'gss' conditional density estimates using inner-validation MSPE weights,
    where MSPE is mean squared prediction error (also known as ISE). The default
    density ratio combines KLIEP and uLSIF estimates with separately
    cross-validated outcome and standardised-covariate bandwidths, followed by
    inner-validation MSPE ensemble weights. Other baseline estimators and
    median-heuristic ratio bandwidths remain available as options. An
    efficient-influence-function estimator of
    the local average treatment
    effect ('SIV_LATE') is provided as a reference point under the standard
    instrumental-variable assumptions.  Instrument, treatment, and
    discrete-outcome nuisance functions are estimated by a Super Learner
    ensemble ('MySL').
